
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good — that keeps the subsystem small.

  Next: how the rest of the app gets at this. "Other forms will need it later" is the requirement that forces this
  choice, and it's the one that touches every file we add from here on.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether the session store is a [3mmodule[0m other code imports, or a [3mglobal[0m other code reaches for. This sets the
  shape of every future file, so it's the expensive one to change later.

  - [1mES modules[0m — a [38;5;153msession.js[39m that other files [38;5;153mimport[39m. Real encapsulation, explicit dependencies, no load-order
    surprises, and browsers run it natively with no build step. One catch worth knowing up front: [38;5;153m<script type="module">
[39m    won't load from a [38;5;153mfile://[39m URL, so opening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m by double-clicking it stops working — you'd need to serve the
    directory ([38;5;153mnpx serve[39m, [38;5;153mpython3 -m http.server[39m, anything).
  - [1mGlobal namespace object[0m — a [38;5;153mwindow.AppSession[39m with get/set/clear, loaded by an extra [38;5;153m<script>[39m tag. Matches the
    current code exactly, keeps [38;5;153mfile://[39m working, zero new concepts. Costs: no encapsulation, and correctness depends on
    script order in the HTML — which is exactly the kind of bug that shows up only once there are several forms.
  - [1mAdd a bundler[0m — npm plus esbuild or Vite. Gives you modules, tests, and a real dev server, and is where this ends up
    if the app keeps growing. But it's a toolchain in a repo that currently has zero dependencies, and it's a lot of
    machinery for one small store.

  My recommendation is ES modules. The requirement you gave — shared across forms that don't exist yet — is precisely
  the one that global load-order gets wrong, and native modules buy the boundary without buying a build step. The
  [38;5;153mfile://[39m tradeoff is the only real cost, and it's reversible.
